// Basic's memory debugger: an allocator that wraps another and records
// every allocation it gives, so a program can learn what it never freed,
// with each allocation's size, its number, and the place it was asked for
// at. A program checks itself by pushing the debugger's allocator in the
// context once, in main (spec 6.13, 7.10): everything that takes its memory
// from the context's allocator then takes it through the debugger, which
// passes each call on to the allocator it wraps, the heap unless another is
// given, and keeps a record of what it gave. memory_debugger_report then
// writes what isn't freed.
//
// The records are kept by the allocation's address in an Int_Table, so a
// free or a resize finds its record at once however many there are. Their
// memory is the heap's, HEAP_ALLOCATOR, whatever the debugger wraps: not
// the context's, since an allocator procedure is given its caller's
// context (spec 7.10), whose allocator is the debugger itself when the
// program pushed it, so the records would be allocations of their own, or
// recurse; and not the wrapped allocator's, since an arena's or the
// temporary storage's FREE_ALL would take them back from under it. Every
// call here that takes an allocator is given one for the same reason, and
// so the allocations the debugger counts are the program's alone.
//
// Each allocation has a number, from 1, in the order the debugger gave
// them, and the place it was asked for at: context.allocation_location
// when the debugger's procedure is called, which Basic's helpers set to the
// place of their call, and Basic's other procedures to the program's own
// call (allocators.rig). The debugger passes each call on by calling the
// wrapped allocator's procedure directly, not through Basic's helpers,
// which would set the place to a line of this file: a direct call leaves
// context.allocation_location as it is, so an arena or a pool it wraps
// passes the program's place on to its parent, and a debugger over the
// debugger records it too.
//
// The debugger changes nothing a program does but refuse its mistakes: a
// call it takes as good reaches the wrapped allocator as the helpers would
// have made it, and gives back what that allocator gives.
#load "allocators.rig";
#load "builder.rig";
#load "table.rig";
#load "sort.rig";
#load "print.rig";

// What the debugger knows of an allocation it gave: its number, from 1 in
// the order they were given; its size in bytes now, after any resize;
// whether it was given back; and the place it was first asked for at,
// zeroed for none.
Memory_Debugger_Record :: struct {
    number: int;
    size: int;
    freed: bool;
    location: Source_Code_Location;
}

// A memory debugger: the allocator it passes calls on to, wrapped; its
// records, Memory_Debugger_Record values by address; the allocations it
// has made, so the last one's number; the bad calls it was given; and
// where it writes, a string builder, or standard output when output is
// null.
Memory_Debugger :: struct {
    wrapped: Allocator;
    records: Int_Table;
    allocations: int;
    bad_calls: int;
    output: *String_Builder;
}

// Makes debugger an empty debugger over wrapped, the context's allocator
// unless given: no allocations and no bad calls, writing to standard
// output, its records an empty table on the heap. Like table_init, it
// doesn't give back records a debugger already had: memory_debugger_free
// does.
memory_debugger_init :: (debugger: *Memory_Debugger, wrapped: Allocator = context.allocator) {
    debugger.wrapped = wrapped;
    int_table_init(*debugger.records, size_of(Memory_Debugger_Record), HEAP_ALLOCATOR);
    debugger.allocations = 0;
    debugger.bad_calls = 0;
    debugger.output = null;
}

// The ending of a count's noun: "" for 1, "s" for any other count, so the
// debugger writes "1 byte" and "2 bytes".
memory_debugger_plural :: (n: int) -> string {
    if n == 1 { return ""; }
    return "s";
}

// Writes a line of the debugger's: at's place first, as PATH:LINE:COL: ,
// when it has one (a line above 0), as a diagnostic or a runtime error
// starts (spec 8.1), and then format with its {}s replaced by args, as
// print does. It writes to standard output when the debugger's output is
// null, as all of Basic does, and otherwise appends to output, whose
// allocator must not be the debugger. Every line the debugger writes goes
// through here, so where it writes is this procedure's choice alone.
//
// print allocates nothing, and print_to_builder takes only the heap's
// memory and output's own allocator's, so writing is no allocation of the
// debugger's.
memory_debugger_write :: (debugger: *Memory_Debugger, at: Source_Code_Location, format: string, args: ..Any) {
    if debugger.output == null {
        if at.line > 0 { print("{}:{}:{}: ", at.file, at.line, at.column); }
        print(format, ..args);
    } else {
        if at.line > 0 { print_to_builder(debugger.output, "{}:{}:{}: ", at.file, at.line, at.column); }
        print_to_builder(debugger.output, format, ..args);
    }
}

// A memory debugger's allocator procedure; data is its *Memory_Debugger.
// Each call is passed on to the wrapped allocator by a direct call of its
// procedure, and settles the edges as Basic's helpers do (allocators.rig),
// so the wrapped allocator is asked only what the helpers would ask it:
//
// - ALLOCATE of 0 bytes or less gives null without asking. Otherwise the
//   wrapped allocator's memory: its null is passed on and isn't counted;
//   anything else is the next allocation, recorded with its size and the
//   place, and returned.
// - RESIZE of null is ALLOCATE. Of an allocation the debugger gave and
//   hasn't taken back, to 0 bytes or less, it is FREE and gives null.
//   Otherwise the resize is passed on, and the wrapped allocator's null is
//   passed on with the record as it was; anything else is the same
//   allocation, its number and place kept, its size the new one, at the
//   result's address. When the result moved, the record at the old
//   address is marked freed.
// - FREE of null does nothing. Of an allocation the debugger gave and
//   hasn't taken back, the record is marked freed, and the free is passed
//   on with its old_size as given: Basic's free passes 0, and a resizable
//   array's free the array's bytes.
// - FREE_ALL is passed on.
//
// A resize or a free of memory the debugger never gave, or has taken back,
// and a resize whose old_size isn't the allocation's size, aren't passed
// on: a resize gives null, which leaves the memory as it was, as the
// protocol says of a null, and a free does nothing.
//
// The place is copied first, before the procedure calls anything of
// Basic's: each of Basic's helpers puts the field back after its own call,
// so the copy only makes plain which place is read.
memory_debugger_allocator_proc :: (mode: int, size: int, old_size: int, old_memory: *void, data: *void) -> *void {
    at := context.allocation_location;
    debugger := cast(*Memory_Debugger) data;
    wrapped := debugger.wrapped;
    if mode == ALLOCATE || (mode == RESIZE && old_memory == null) {
        if size <= 0 { return null; }
        memory := wrapped.proc(ALLOCATE, size, 0, null, wrapped.data);
        if memory == null { return null; }
        debugger.allocations += 1;
        record := Memory_Debugger_Record.{debugger.allocations, size, false, at};
        int_table_set(*debugger.records, cast(int) memory, *record);
        return memory;
    }
    if mode == RESIZE {
        found := cast(*Memory_Debugger_Record) int_table_find(debugger.records, cast(int) old_memory);
        if found == null || found.freed { return null; }
        if size <= 0 {
            found.freed = true;
            wrapped.proc(FREE, 0, 0, old_memory, wrapped.data);
            return null;
        }
        if old_size != found.size { return null; }
        // The record is copied out before the resize is passed on, and the
        // old one is found again after it: a set may rehash the table and
        // move every record, the debugger's own below, and any allocation
        // the wrapped allocator makes through the context while it resizes,
        // whose allocator is this debugger when the program pushed it.
        record := found.*;
        memory := wrapped.proc(RESIZE, size, old_size, old_memory, wrapped.data);
        if memory == null { return null; }
        if memory != old_memory {
            moved := cast(*Memory_Debugger_Record) int_table_find(debugger.records, cast(int) old_memory);
            moved.freed = true;
        }
        record.size = size;
        int_table_set(*debugger.records, cast(int) memory, *record);
        return memory;
    }
    if mode == FREE {
        if old_memory == null { return null; }
        found := cast(*Memory_Debugger_Record) int_table_find(debugger.records, cast(int) old_memory);
        if found == null || found.freed { return null; }
        found.freed = true;
        wrapped.proc(FREE, 0, old_size, old_memory, wrapped.data);
        return null;
    }
    if mode == FREE_ALL {
        wrapped.proc(FREE_ALL, 0, 0, null, wrapped.data);
    }
    return null;
}

// debugger as an Allocator.
memory_debugger_allocator :: (debugger: *Memory_Debugger) -> Allocator {
    return Allocator.{memory_debugger_allocator_proc, debugger};
}

// Whether the record at a goes before the one at b, by number, for sort.
memory_debugger_less :: (a: *void, b: *void) -> bool {
    return (cast(*Memory_Debugger_Record) a).number < (cast(*Memory_Debugger_Record) b).number;
}

// Writes what debugger gave and hasn't taken back, one line per
// allocation, at the place it was asked for at, in the order they were
// given, then a line that counts the allocations, those not freed and
// their bytes, and the bad calls; and gives whether every allocation is
// freed and there was no bad call. The program chooses what to do with the
// answer, such as its exit status. It may be called any number of times:
// a report before the end is a checkpoint, and the numbering goes on.
//
// The table's order is the addresses' hashes', which differ from run to
// run, so the records not freed are copied out and sorted by number
// first, in memory from the heap, given back before it returns.
memory_debugger_report :: (debugger: *Memory_Debugger) -> bool {
    records := debugger.records;
    live := cast(*Memory_Debugger_Record) alloc(records.count * size_of(Memory_Debugger_Record), HEAP_ALLOCATOR);
    count := 0;
    bytes := 0;
    for 0..records.capacity - 1 {
        used, key, value := int_table_entry(records, it);
        if used {
            record := cast(*Memory_Debugger_Record) value;
            if !record.freed {
                live[count] = record.*;
                count += 1;
                bytes += record.size;
            }
        }
    }
    sort(live, count, size_of(Memory_Debugger_Record), memory_debugger_less, HEAP_ALLOCATOR);
    for 0..count - 1 {
        record := live[it];
        memory_debugger_write(debugger, record.location, "memory debugger: allocation {} ({} byte{}) isn't freed\n", record.number, record.size, memory_debugger_plural(record.size));
    }
    free(live, HEAP_ALLOCATOR);
    none : Source_Code_Location;
    memory_debugger_write(debugger, none, "memory debugger: {} allocation{}, {} not freed ({} byte{}), {} bad call{}\n", debugger.allocations, memory_debugger_plural(debugger.allocations), count, bytes, memory_debugger_plural(bytes), debugger.bad_calls, memory_debugger_plural(debugger.bad_calls));
    return count == 0 && debugger.bad_calls == 0;
}

// Gives debugger's records back to the heap, and nothing it tracked: what
// it gave is the wrapped allocator's, the program's to give back. The
// debugger forgets every allocation; memory_debugger_init makes it ready
// again.
memory_debugger_free :: (debugger: *Memory_Debugger) {
    int_table_free(*debugger.records);
}
